Description: avformat/mpeg, avformat/mpegenc: stamp DVD PCM packets by their first byte
 The PES timestamp of a DVD-Video LPCM packet names the first audio frame
 that starts in its payload, and the packet's first access unit pointer
 says where that frame starts: 4 is the payload's first byte. The
 demuxer skipped the pointer and gave the timestamp to the payload's
 first byte. Each packet was stamped late by the bytes in front of its
 first frame, up to 1/600 s on a disc.
 .
 The muxer wrote 7 frames and pointer 4 into every packet. Its
 timestamp is that of the first encoder frame after the rest of the
 frame before, so the pointer was false whenever a payload started with
 such a rest. A reader is then late by up to one encoder frame: 3.5 ms
 for 24-bit stereo at 48 kHz, 20.9 ms for 16-bit mono. When that rest
 filled the payload once it was cut to whole blocks, the packet carried
 the timestamp of a frame that starts in a later packet.
 .
 The demuxer now moves the timestamp of a DVD PCM packet back by the
 bytes in front of its pointer. The muxer writes the number of frames
 that start in the payload and the true pointer, or 0 and no timestamp
 when none starts.
Author: Streamarr
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-11
